  Re-read the Guidebook!  It came with your game.  Or you can read
and/or download it from <http://www.nethack.org>.  It's very long,
but it's far and away the best way to give yourself a leg up at this 
point, without being spoiled.


  Practical advice:

  Test every non-cursed piece of armor you find for enchantment, 
weight, and total AC reduction.  (But that's not all that's important
about armor, as you'll eventually find out.)

  Lightweight armor is good, protection is better.  There are few
better places to allocate carrying capacity than to protection.

  Really test gloves, boots, shields and helms.  You'll see a lot more
of such "accessory" armor, so you're more likely to find some with
higher enchantments.  A couple of +3 enchantments and you're in pretty
good shape.  (Early in the game finding a single +3 enchantment can
keep your character alive.)

  Armor testing is the one thing an early character can do without 
much risk.  (Although you may need a little spoiling to learn how.)


  Each role (monk, wizard, valkyrie, priest, ranger, rogue, knight,
etc.) generally has both a strength and a weakness.  Finding those out
on your own may be impossible.  It's also nearly impossible to play 
most roles well without knowing those strengths/weaknesses.

